A||N||24||bHooper||glowing hot manifold||||4||
Z||000000||bHooper||05-15-2000||10:10 PM||||i cranked up my car, to let it run some tonight, and my buddy notice that the rear manifold was glowing!!!!<P>help!!<P>what could be the problem here?<P>thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||4
Z||000001||GT Bastard||05-15-2000||10:19 PM||||check timing!||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||05-15-2000||10:26 PM||||If the timing is too far advanced, the car will run hotter, and it will also idle faster. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||05-15-2000||10:39 PM||||The exhaust manifolds on both my Fieros glow red when first started.  I never had noticed it until I was working at dusk and there was enough contrast to see a dull red on both the front and rear manifolds.  I got nervous and check #2 Fiero and saw the same thing.  Except for some melted sparkplug wires they havent been a problem.  (I got a better grade of wires and installed the heat shields)||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||05-15-2000||10:45 PM||||ok, so how do i check the timeing?<P>this is something i have never done.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||GT Bastard||05-15-2000||11:03 PM||||First off, get a timing light.  Set the car in diagnostic mode by removing the black plastic plate that surround the lighter, then use a paperclip or piece of wire to jumper the two pins in the upper left corner of that adsl connector(you'll see what I mean when you remove the plate.)  when I say upper left, I mean the two pins on top closest to the passenger seat.  Viola, now you're in diagnostic mode.  Now start the car.  It'll probably idle like crap, which is normal.  Then connect the timing light and point it at the timing marks above the crank pulley.  If I'm correct, the timing mark on the crank pulley should appear to hover around the second notch on the left if you're standing in front of the pulleys.  Man, this is difficult to explain.  Anyhow, you can adjust the timing by loosening the distributor hold-down plate and turning the distributor slightly if the timing is incorrect.  Clockwise should be retarted, and counter-clockwise should be advanced.  I'm at work right now so I don't have my manual, but somebody will correct me if I got anything wrong.  This is how I did my timing though, and it's dead-on.  Definitely get a shop manual if you don't have one yet. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fiasco||05-15-2000||11:36 PM||||You want to jumper the LOWER two terminals on the passenger side. The top two are empty-<BR>        ---------------<BR>       |= = = = = = =||+ + = = = = =|<BR>        ---------------<P>And you average the readings for the #1 and #4 cylinders and that should be 10 deg. BTDC.<BR>All this should be on the VECI label on the underside of the deck lid.<p>[This message has been edited by Fiasco (edited 05-15-2000).]||209.224.19.179||reg||2
Z||000007||GT Bastard||05-16-2000||12:09 AM||||I think you're right Fiasco. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fiasco||05-16-2000||12:21 AM||||Yep.<BR>Hope this helps, Hoop.||||reg||12
Z||000009||bHooper||05-16-2000||06:50 AM||||i am (i believe) in a total state of awe.  but i am sure that i will figure out how to do it, once i start.<P>so, can anyone explain to me how this causes the manifold to glow?<P>thank everyone,<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000010||bHooper||05-16-2000||01:58 PM||||just curious... after talking with my father, who has some mechanical experience... wouldnt i have had to done something, to knock the timeing off?  i have not touched the ignition system (other than replacing the plugs and wires) and the car sounds fine (other than idleing at 2500 rpms).<P>George, also suggested that the engine could be dumping too much fuel, i have noticed a sizeable amount of fuel consumption (for a car setting on jack stands), but have not witnessed any funny smoke out the back.<P>I have purchased a new o2 sensor, and will replace mine.  I will still go ahead and check the timeing.<P>As for a plugged catalitic convertor or muffler, if I disconected (or losened) the y pipe beside the slave bracket, that should eliminate that (if it continues to glow, and the above prove useless).<P>Once I have done the 3 steps above, and have no success, what should I look for next?  Is it possible that carbon deposits from the cylinders could cause something like this?<P>thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000011||DRH||05-16-2000||02:06 PM||||Check and/or re-check for a vacuum leak. That can cause hot manifolds AND a high idle.<P>EGR tube is a likely culprit. I don't remember what all you were working on. Double check anything you worked on that could cause a vacuum leak though...<P>||||reg||3
Z||000012||bHooper||05-16-2000||02:26 PM||||all i did was pull the engine to reinstall a tranny.<P>of course, it is a 14 year old car, i guess the vacume lines could be rotten and decaying.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000013||bHooper||05-16-2000||02:27 PM||||after checking the timing, tonite, i will pull the plenum and examine every vacume line.  <P>is there a easy way to check for leaks?<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000014||terryk||05-16-2000||07:12 PM||||Spary carb cleaner on ports and lines. If there is a leak it will be sucked into the leak and the engine will stumble. You will many times see a puff of smoke from the exhaust as it is burn. Water will make it stumble too. Not as much unless the leak is real bad and you really spray it.||||reg||1
Z||000015||fiero56||05-16-2000||08:19 PM||||Make sure the gaskets for the plenum are good, and that you have torqued the silver bolts that hold the plenum on to spec. I had a high idle after replacing my clutch, and it turned out one of my new plenum gaskets was missing some material. As far as rich/lean problems, the engine would be running lean to make those manifolds glow. <P>I didn't know running rich would cause them to also?||||reg||1
Z||000016||Dan||05-16-2000||08:27 PM|||| Check every vaccum line, under the cruise control,cannister,all lines and connections.Vacuum leak makes the engine burn lean.A rich burning engine runs cooler,and lean is hotter.Conversley,by the timing being off,the engine recreates conditions of a lean burn in the cylinder.Between those two things,it should take care of your glowing manifolds.||||reg||1
Z||000017||bHooper||05-16-2000||09:36 PM||||i still dont know what the problem is.  but honestly, i didnt have a lot of time to work on it tonight.  <P>i replaced the o2 sensor, checked the codes.  got a 23, 24 and a 35.  disconected the battery to be sure, restarted it and got nothing, just the 12.<P>as for the timing, is it possible to nock it out of adjustment on its own?  i did nothing to the ignition, myself.<P>i am dismantleing the plenum, so that i can start looking at the vacume lines... what is the proper torque to use when bolting it back together?<P>if i need to replace the gaskets, where can i  get those? NAPA?<P>thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000018||bHooper||05-17-2000||07:23 AM||||i dont know if this makes a difference... but i only have the <B>one</B> manifold glowing!  the one that faces the passenger compartment.||||reg||1
Z||000019||rogergarrison||05-17-2000||07:53 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by bHooper:<BR>[B]i cranked up my car, to let it run some tonight, and my buddy notice that the rear manifold was glowing!!!!<P>i had same problem, found that the large vacume line running from pvc valve, under the intake plenum to a nipple underneath behind the distributor(nipple is on bottom side of plenum) had come off. I made new line from fuel line and clamped it on instead of the push fit. Also made idle drop over 500 rpms.  <p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 05-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||bHooper||05-17-2000||08:54 AM||||roger, did you just have the <B>one</B> manifold glowing?  if so, which one?<P>i am in the slow and painfull process of dismantling everything to investigate the vacume situation.<P><B>i do feel a lot of hot air blowing</B> around the throttle cable connection on the throttle body!  but i plan to check and replace everything that looks suspect.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000021||bHooper||05-17-2000||09:50 PM||||i spent another 3.5 hours working on this problem, tonight, and here is my take on the <B>whole</B> situation.<P> removeed all of the vacume lines, and they seem to be fine... reinstalled everything, the car still idled at 2000 rpms, and the manifold still glowed.<P>my dad gave me his <B>old</B> timeing gun, last used in 1983.  but never haveing done this, i would have had better luck trying to land Eagle 1 on the moon!<P>i still had hot air comeing up near the throttle body... the crossover wasnt tightened all that well to the CC.  fixed that, eliminated <B>some</B> of the hot air.<P>cranked the car up, still glowing... get pissed, start punching the accelerator.  whats this, the idle dropped to 1400 rpms?<P>i turn all the lights off, and to my amazement... both manifolds are glowing!<P>guess what?  i am not worried any more!  why?  well, lets see, i have a 14 yo car that hasnt cranked in 4 years.  its setting in my garage, and the front is on jack stands and the engine is idleing 500-1000 rpms too much.<P>it all makes perfect sense to me now... it just isnt getting that cool fresh air to the engine compartment!  think about it!  now im not saying that the timeing couldnt be off a little (i will PAY someone to check this for me).  its also possible that the coolent is recerculating all that well, as the car isnt level.<P>anyway, at this point, i am ready to just slap on the slave cylinder (if and when it ever arrives), and go for a drive around the block!<P>thanks for allowing me to vent!<BR>and by all means, feel free to offer any further bits of knowledge.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000022||bHooper||05-18-2000||07:40 AM||||well, after all that work last night, guess what i forgot to do?<P><BR>thats right...<P><BR>brian forgot to bolt the egr tube to the egr valve!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> <P>i was looking at the thing, this morn, and that tube stuck out like a big sore!  hooked it up, and the car is idleing between 900-1100 RPM!!!!!! YEA!!!!<P>i didnt let it run long enough to see how the manifold looked, but it whould be a lot better now!  it does sound a little rough, still.<P>things are getting better!<P>hoop||||reg||11
